<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yamuna River, Jumna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yamuna is a major river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, flowing about 1,370 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> to join the Ganges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>. The fertile Ganga–Yamuna doab between the two rivers was the heartland of many north Indian states. The river is sacred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and closely linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamuna.jpg" alt="Yamuna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Umang108 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishram_Ghat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
